insert into public.tips (id, unit_id, title_en, body_md, status) values
  ('e9493171-429d-5166-8af8-a23846e6f240', 'ecf3c9a9-33e3-5e2e-b2b7-aa09f082c128', 'Otro, never un otro', 'To order the same again, say **otro**: *Otro café, por favor.* For a word that takes *una*, it''s **otra**: *Otra cerveza.* It already means "another", so nothing goes in front.', 'published'),
  ('fbdb815c-fea1-52ae-84c1-af98b2814484', 'ecf3c9a9-33e3-5e2e-b2b7-aa09f082c128', 'Un or una', 'The little word matches the thing, not you: **un** té, **un** helado, but **una** torta, **una** pizza. Learn each new word with its *un* or *una* and you''ll never have to guess.', 'published'),
  ('bc1f8253-cff9-5535-923b-d4d417fb4742', '1f792bd1-c22b-5919-b21f-f22c96ecef14', 'Buen día', 'In Buenos Aires the morning greeting is **buen día**, until lunch. After that it''s **buenas tardes**, and once it''s dark **buenas noches** — hello *and* goodbye. Short for any of them: **buenas**.', 'draft'),
  ('73cc59dc-ddb9-5779-b542-a35b9fd331ff', '1f792bd1-c22b-5919-b21f-f22c96ecef14', 'Gracias, de nada', 'When someone says *gracias*, answer **de nada**. *Of course* is **claro**. And when something''s sorted, porteños say **¡joya!** — *¿Todo bien? — ¡Joya!*', 'draft'),
  ('4f51caae-78e5-509e-a8ea-9171010db452', '8ee9449f-672c-5e88-91cb-6db07525f370', 'Soy turista — no un', 'With *ser*, a role goes straight after the verb: **Soy estudiante.** **¿Sos turista?** English says *a student*; Spanish leaves the *un* out.', 'draft'),
  ('42b7a8c7-d550-5297-a8c7-d7d165cb4dd9', '8ee9449f-672c-5e88-91cb-6db07525f370', '¿Sos vos?', '**Vos** can come after the verb when you''re checking who someone is: *Ah, ¿sos vos?* — *Oh, it''s you?* The answer: **Sí, soy yo.** Can''t believe it? **¿Posta?** — *for real?*', 'draft'),
  ('22d2fbbf-280c-57ce-81c6-2c082146f720', '8ee9449f-672c-5e88-91cb-6db07525f370', 'Bienvenido, bienvenida', 'Someone new in town gets a **¡bienvenido!** — or **¡bienvenida!** for a woman. The end of the word matches the person.', 'draft'),
  ('6620174b-7e7e-5f88-a3b7-17aa03b97032', 'c5846290-e8b5-5d22-8910-aae061dcf004', 'Soy, sos', 'Two forms of *ser* so far: **soy** for yourself, **sos** for the person in front of you. *Soy Lucía, ¿y vos?* — *¿Sos turista?* The pronoun is optional; the verb already says who.', 'draft'),
  ('e2dfe789-76c6-566c-a709-002aaaf789fa', 'c5846290-e8b5-5d22-8910-aae061dcf004', 'The right hello', '**Buen día** in the morning, **buenas tardes** later, **buenas** any time. To leave: **chau** or **hasta luego**. And after every *gracias*, a **de nada**.', 'draft'),
  ('dce725f6-bc10-516a-85c8-df10e2a4e81f', 'eba40ebe-93cf-52fb-a853-9a2836300617', 'Encantado, encantada', '**Encantado** — *delighted* — is what you say when you meet someone, and it matches whoever is speaking: a man says *encantado*, a woman **encantada**. The reply: **igualmente** — *likewise*.', 'draft'),
  ('28a935f7-a31d-590c-bc93-64b6bd4b0b21', 'eba40ebe-93cf-52fb-a853-9a2836300617', '-o for me, -ás or -és for vos', 'Other verbs work like *me llamo, te llamás*: **hablo**, **¿hablás?** — **entiendo**, **¿entendés?** The *I* form ends in **-o**; the vos form is stressed on the end. Lost? *Perdón, no entiendo. **Despacio**, por favor.*', 'draft'),
  ('8f1d1346-2aba-5b40-a0f6-366a54fd2257', 'eba40ebe-93cf-52fb-a853-9a2836300617', 'Nombre y apellido', 'At any counter in Buenos Aires you''ll hear *nombre y apellido, por favor* — your first name and your surname.', 'draft'),
  ('365922d8-9f57-5d59-8640-f9a66dff0181', 'b900b7fe-a570-5688-8aa9-d35d025c5c42', 'De, for any size of place', '*Ser de* works for a country, a city or one corner of it: **Soy de un pueblo de Córdoba.** **Soy de zona sur.** *Zona norte*, *zona sur* and *zona oeste* take no extra word.', 'draft'),
  ('42534aab-2616-5cd9-8ce8-366b4feba608', 'b900b7fe-a570-5688-8aa9-d35d025c5c42', 'Capital', 'Porteños call the city itself **Capital** — *Soy de Capital* — to set it apart from the suburbs around it. Not from here at all? **Soy de afuera.**', 'draft'),
  ('818d8222-0b07-51a2-8691-eb2d6fe9245f', 'd5d88ab0-8bb3-5ef0-9e7c-8c83d78b32ae', 'Se llama', 'For someone else the little word is **se**: *me llamo*, *te llamás*, **se llama**. *¿Cómo se llama él?* — *Se llama Juan.*', 'draft'),
  ('fde51102-e5bd-5f88-9d8d-8656cc371174', 'd5d88ab0-8bb3-5ef0-9e7c-8c83d78b32ae', '¿Quién es?', '**Quién** asks *who*: *¿Quién es él?* The answer drops the pronoun: *Es Martín, un vecino.* A **pibe** is a guy and a **piba** a girl — any age, very porteño.', 'draft'),
  ('59176d42-3da8-55af-80e9-e62ad6a9d0d5', 'd5d88ab0-8bb3-5ef0-9e7c-8c83d78b32ae', 'También, tampoco', '**Tampoco** is the *no* version of *también*: *Él no es de acá, y ella tampoco.* — *He''s not from here, and neither is she.*', 'draft'),
  ('5abd2580-993b-5867-b0dc-d00214d7a710', 'd3b8a1a7-a655-577c-8297-f7f1ff9d08cd', 'Soy, sos, es', 'Three people now: **soy** de acá, **¿sos** de Capital?, **es** de afuera. Name, origin, role — *ser* carries all of them.', 'draft'),
  ('b1aa4169-fdbd-58b4-8a29-71a33002b0fb', 'd3b8a1a7-a655-577c-8297-f7f1ff9d08cd', 'Me, te, se', 'The name verb changes its little word with the person: **me llamo**, **te llamás**, **se llama**.', 'draft'),
  ('4416a0d4-5205-5005-94af-d90daa90a970', '9a6d16d1-11b7-53bd-b0b9-676b76d00eb2', 'Three kinds of ending', '**-o** turns into **-a**: *brasileño, brasileña*. A consonant adds **-a**: *español, española*; *francés, francesa* (the accent goes). A word in **-e** never changes: *canadiense*, *estadounidense* — for a man or a woman.', 'draft'),
  ('afd99159-1ede-599a-b6f1-a58bd49520a1', '9a6d16d1-11b7-53bd-b0b9-676b76d00eb2', 'Small letters', 'Nationalities are written in lowercase: *Ella es* **italiana**, never *Italiana*. Only the country takes a capital: *Brasil*, *Estados Unidos*.', 'draft'),
  ('0ff08a63-4397-541c-ab52-b49288a29d28', 'd8b23128-fa60-5c82-9370-1cad2fff44fc', 'Tío, tía', 'Most of the family comes in *-o / -a* pairs: **tío, tía** — **primo, prima** — **sobrino, sobrina** — **nieto, nieta**. *Mi* stays the same for all of them.', 'draft'),
  ('fe0d5efc-b8e4-5065-82e5-0d79dc803125', 'd8b23128-fa60-5c82-9370-1cad2fff44fc', 'Mi mujer, mi marido', 'Porteños say **mi mujer** for *my wife* and **mi marido** for *my husband* — two different words, not a pair. And **bebé** is one word for a baby boy or girl: *un bebé*.', 'draft'),
  ('f06b4712-f82e-59d7-833a-ebc743433d6d', 'a48206a5-01f1-5f99-a064-2112a1860df7', 'You have hunger', 'Where English *is*, Spanish *has*: **tengo hambre** (I''m hungry), **tengo sed** (thirsty), **tengo frío**, **tengo calor**, **tengo sueño** (sleepy), **tengo miedo** (scared). The same *tener* as your age.', 'draft'),
  ('a1fe4a81-9b70-5554-9579-935a484b2d87', 'a48206a5-01f1-5f99-a064-2112a1860df7', 'Tenés razón', '**Tenés razón** — *you''re right*, literally *you have reason*. **No tengo tiempo** — *I don''t have time*. **Tiene suerte** — *he''s lucky*.', 'draft'),
  ('a58b0d57-626c-5e35-a30b-0557783b49d8', '57ee5db8-0033-56bd-b766-c351ed945cfb', 'Tengo, tenés, tiene', 'One verb for your age and for how you feel: **tengo** veinte años, **¿tenés** frío?, mi tío **tiene** sueño.', 'draft'),
  ('d244b49a-e1eb-5cb3-9ae8-b0efee8aa45d', '57ee5db8-0033-56bd-b766-c351ed945cfb', '-o, -a, or no change', 'Words for people follow the person: *mi tío, mi tía*; *brasileño, brasileña*. A word in *-e* stays put: *canadiense*.', 'draft'),
  ('1c3decf9-4230-5b82-8977-3554b239262d', 'bc934576-e1db-5554-93e2-cec9b7738349', '-s or -es', 'A word ending in a vowel adds **-s**: *galletita → galletitas*, *chicle → chicles*. One ending in a consonant adds **-es**: *alfajor → **alfajores***. With a number, *un* and *una* drop out: *dos paquetes*.', 'draft'),
  ('150987e8-e0fb-5adf-b51a-24fa8e5d5436', 'bc934576-e1db-5554-93e2-cec9b7738349', 'Tu, tus — mi, mis', '*Your* is **tu**, and it takes an *-s* with more than one: **tu** hermano, **tus** hermanos. *My* works the same way: *mi* gato, **mis** gatos.', 'draft'),
  ('c99796c8-7e24-5a5d-ab39-3c8321dd111c', 'bc934576-e1db-5554-93e2-cec9b7738349', 'Alfajores', 'An **alfajor** is two soft biscuits stuck together with *dulce de leche*, often dipped in chocolate. Every kiosco sells them, and every porteño has a favourite.', 'draft'),
  ('a2996575-54ad-5719-978d-eea25982d947', 'fd0199e1-0dc9-5704-8917-1613531c1778', 'Son altos', 'What people are like goes with *ser*: **somos altos**, **son morochos**. The word takes an *-s* for more than one, and *-os* covers a mixed group: *mis hermanos son altos*, even if one of them is a sister.', 'draft'),
  ('7d0417aa-4dc3-51d7-8a9a-5889882ac559', 'fd0199e1-0dc9-5704-8917-1613531c1778', 'Morocho, rubio, flaco', 'Porteños describe people by the obvious and nobody minds: **morocho** is dark-haired, **rubio** fair, **flaco** thin, **petiso** short. *Flaco* is even a nickname: *¿Todo bien, flaco?*', 'draft'),
  ('4a1d6bf6-aed0-55e3-83e7-5fe81b444a4e', 'fd0199e1-0dc9-5704-8917-1613531c1778', 'Soy soltero, son casados', 'Single or married goes with *ser* too: **soy soltera**, **son casados**. You''ll also hear *estoy casado* — both are everyday Buenos Aires.', 'draft'),
  ('1951eb4e-526e-5cea-b4e7-d2477eb2b5d3', '9c3ea7c9-a129-5157-b092-804da692c36c', 'Está, están', 'One thing **está**, more than one **están**: *¿Dónde está mi celu?* — *¿Dónde están mis anteojos?* The answer drops the thing you''re looking for: **Están en la mochila.**', 'draft'),
  ('0f108707-6528-5552-95c5-9202ab24e0d7', '9c3ea7c9-a129-5157-b092-804da692c36c', 'Arriba, abajo, adentro', 'Three words that need nothing after them: **arriba** (up, upstairs), **abajo** (down, downstairs), **adentro** (inside). *Estoy abajo* is what a porteño texts from the street when they''re at your door.', 'draft'),
  ('4a5c7164-2735-57f0-a2e7-64e4f99cc52c', '9c3ea7c9-a129-5157-b092-804da692c36c', 'En casa', 'No *la* before home: **estoy en casa**, *I''m at home*. With anything else the little word comes back: *en la mochila*, *en el auto*.', 'draft'),
  ('f82e713f-35bd-5bcc-9129-df81689de3cb', '6d6f0271-451e-511a-ae75-885699df6981', 'Por acá', '**Por acá** is *around here*, the end of every question on the street: *¿Hay un bar por acá?* *¿Hay un taxi por acá?* The answer is usually a finger and *a dos cuadras*.', 'draft'),
  ('b861d84d-53e2-566d-9424-5b40732e4523', '6d6f0271-451e-511a-ae75-885699df6981', 'Hay gente', '**Hay** works for things you can''t count, too: **hay gente** — there are people. *Gente* is singular in Spanish: *la gente está en la plaza*.', 'draft'),
  ('6c4ee507-763d-5871-85e7-e0fdacd5e974', '6d6f0271-451e-511a-ae75-885699df6981', 'Al parque, del subte', 'The same two joins as before: *a la plaza* but **al** parque, **al** hospital; *la estación **del** subte*, *la parada **del** bondi*.', 'draft'),
  ('ad86f8a0-5288-5bfb-91b5-2f307f1ccce2', 'edbefa4a-ecf6-5ba9-8f18-56b44e86a423', 'Son or están', '**Son** says what people are like: *son altos*, *son simpáticos*. **Están** says where they are: *están en la plaza*. The same people, two verbs.', 'draft'),
  ('0ffd37eb-c87d-5d8a-b831-e762f82fa1c6', 'edbefa4a-ecf6-5ba9-8f18-56b44e86a423', 'Hay or está', '**Hay** for something new: *hay una estación por acá*. **Está** for the one you already mean: *la estación está en la avenida*.', 'draft'),
  ('c78965a3-c86d-5ee5-ab88-f0da832aa2fe', 'ef2cae6b-ed9c-5d4b-9d7e-7c65c58dbea5', 'Estar for how things are', '*Estar* isn''t only for people: things have a state right now too. **El bar está cerrado**, **el bondi está lleno**, **la cerveza está caliente**. The word matches the thing: *el bar* está llen**o**, *la plaza* está llen**a**.', 'draft'),
  ('fc0b4b4e-f184-5ab8-adcb-2352ae9cdeae', 'ef2cae6b-ed9c-5d4b-9d7e-7c65c58dbea5', '¿Listo? Listo.', '**Listo** is *ready* — *¿estás lista?* — and on its own it closes any deal: *Listo, dale.* **Apurado** is *in a hurry*, the normal state of anyone in the centro.', 'draft'),
  ('19fdd686-0f86-5a0c-a998-5e353ecd8fd9', 'ef2cae6b-ed9c-5d4b-9d7e-7c65c58dbea5', 'Caliente', '**Caliente** is for things: *el café está caliente*. Said about a person it means angry — or something ruder — so for the weather or yourself, use *tengo calor*.', 'draft'),
  ('70bc28a3-d94c-55ce-acbc-81ab243359c6', '7f39f4c5-591c-51f7-9f82-ca7f7a1cfb2b', 'The same five endings', 'Every new verb here is a regular -ar verb, so you already know them: *practic**o**, practic**ás**, practic**a**, practic**amos***. Learn the infinitive and the rest comes free: *necesito, usás, ayuda, enseñan*.', 'draft'),
  ('c2a5f3ac-9d60-5f16-b276-32fe3605b2f1', '7f39f4c5-591c-51f7-9f82-ca7f7a1cfb2b', 'Buscar, preguntar', '**Buscar** is *to look for* — no *for* needed: *busco una palabra*. **Preguntar** is *to ask* a question: *pregunto por qué*. Neither takes an extra word in front of the thing.', 'draft'),
  ('96e834f8-6753-5479-8174-299e06ce61b8', '7f39f4c5-591c-51f7-9f82-ca7f7a1cfb2b', 'Más despacio', 'Porteños talk **rápido**. The line that saves you: *¿Podés hablar más despacio?* — and most people will happily slow down and **ayudar**.', 'draft'),
  ('0ae59745-c14f-5c5a-8d15-5c6ce45c37ad', '5269d1e2-0b24-51b5-b272-6da95a4cc1b0', 'Nos gusta, les gusta', 'The little word changes with the people who like it: **nos** gusta — we like it; **les** gusta — they like it, or you all do. The verb still matches the food: *nos gusta el pollo*, *nos gust**an** los fideos*.', 'draft'),
  ('bcba0543-16e7-5c62-92af-66b9058b7c13', '5269d1e2-0b24-51b5-b272-6da95a4cc1b0', 'A ellos les gusta', '*Les* can be *them* or *you all*, so porteños often say who: **a ellos** les gusta, **a ustedes** les gusta. The same with *le*: *a ella le gusta el pescado*.', 'draft'),
  ('52a41619-5364-53ac-8a49-0b407622963c', '5269d1e2-0b24-51b5-b272-6da95a4cc1b0', 'Los fideos, la verdura', 'Pasta is **los fideos**, always plural. **La verdura** is vegetables in general: *no le gusta la verdura*.', 'draft'),
  ('1f297d17-80f0-51a1-b06f-6a3cab920a9b', '3c53d5f7-3d49-5265-8058-ba19fab0b788', 'Three verbs, three rules', '*Estar* and its word match the person: *estamos cansad**os***. An -ar verb takes the person''s ending: *practic**amos***. *Gustar* matches the thing: *nos gust**an** los fideos*.', 'draft'),
  ('a7aca477-d93f-5ae8-8217-3f52071a1d8f', '3c53d5f7-3d49-5265-8058-ba19fab0b788', 'Me, te, le, nos, les', 'With *gustar* the person goes in front: **me** gusta, **te** gusta, **le** gusta, **nos** gusta, **les** gusta. Only *gusta* or *gustan* ever changes after it.', 'draft'),
  ('bce85693-14c0-55df-9f48-e478356528a2', '62bf976e-3f47-5cc9-b8bb-82f1b5c654b2', 'Quiere, queremos, quieren', 'Ordering for more than one: **queremos** dos cortados. The mozo asks the table **¿qué quieren?** And for someone else: *ella **quiere** un té*. The *ie* is everywhere except *querés* and *queremos*.', 'draft'),
  ('a45d118b-9ee3-50d8-8429-249547ef9eb7', '62bf976e-3f47-5cc9-b8bb-82f1b5c654b2', '¿Nos traés…?', 'Swap *me* for **nos** and the whole table orders at once: **¿Nos traés la carta?** — *¿Nos traés dos vasos, por favor?*', 'draft'),
  ('899d348a-60ee-5d03-bc6d-af23743c92be', '62bf976e-3f47-5cc9-b8bb-82f1b5c654b2', 'Soda y picada', '**Soda** is fizzy water, still served from a siphon in the old cafés. A **picada** is a board of cheese and cold cuts to share before dinner.', 'draft'),
  ('c1c004e5-a9f3-57bb-8d60-abb3553b6823', 'feb8548b-0ed1-59d1-b3d8-a983690a21d1', 'No un, no una', 'The same rule as *soy profesora*: **es enfermera**, **soy contador** — no *un*, no *una*. The article only comes back when you add something: *es una periodista argentina*.', 'draft'),
  ('f3e09f0e-6ae3-5a71-8232-f3379e01c0df', 'feb8548b-0ed1-59d1-b3d8-a983690a21d1', '-o, -or, -ista', '**Cocinero → cocinera**, **vendedor → vendedora**. Words in **-ista** don''t change: *el periodista, la periodista*. And **policía** is the same word for both.', 'draft'),
  ('3910aabd-7879-56ce-9fd0-298db2aac86c', 'feb8548b-0ed1-59d1-b3d8-a983690a21d1', 'Tenemos que, tienen que', '*Tener que* works with every person: *tenemos que laburar*, *los médicos tienen que ayudar*. After it, **buscar** is also *to pick someone up*: *tengo que buscar a mi hijo*.', 'draft'),
  ('17022bbc-7c2a-5dc5-afe6-20e71e1f3d81', 'a47573dd-a997-5b77-acd6-3b206415f578', '-er or -ir: two places to tell', '-er and -ir verbs only differ for **vos** and **nosotros**: *vendés, vendemos* but *compartís, compartimos*. Everywhere else they match: *come, comparte*; *comen, leen, escriben*.', 'draft'),
  ('5f609aca-4b7c-5585-9e3f-2d151bb4c25c', 'a47573dd-a997-5b77-acd6-3b206415f578', 'Abre, recibe', '**Abrir** and **recibir** are regular: *abro, abrís, abre*; *recibo, recibís, recibe*. *¿El negocio abre a la noche?*', 'draft'),
  ('9769b812-efcf-50e2-8dd0-7a43d62e3f1b', 'a47573dd-a997-5b77-acd6-3b206415f578', 'El negocio', '**Negocio** is any shop, not just a business: *el negocio de la esquina*. And porteños **comparten** everything, from a flat to a mate.', 'draft'),
  ('63ff509a-3d7b-5687-a565-dd402adb7b88', '7530c631-f8fc-54fb-a160-bf7d158f1f86', 'Everyone ends in -n', 'The *ellos* form of every verb you have ends in **-n**: *quieren, tienen, laburan, viven, escriben*. Hear the *-n* and you know it''s more than one.', 'draft'),
  ('5cf8649b-a701-532f-ab70-90863e7dfe69', '7530c631-f8fc-54fb-a160-bf7d158f1f86', 'Es cocinero', 'Jobs take no *un*: **es cocinero**, **soy contadora**. The word changes for a woman — *cocinera* — except the *-ista* ones: *periodista*.', 'draft'),
  ('9acb02d2-6b21-5158-ad22-8d29cf9e3a7f', '9ed0dbbb-2500-5957-b946-27d34bce9614', 'Tiene ascensor', 'When you just say whether it has one, no *un*: *mi edificio **tiene** ascensor*, *no **tiene** terraza*. **Hay** for what''s there: *en la planta baja **hay** un patio*.', 'draft'),
  ('c538de60-17a3-5573-95d5-96e0be486781', '9ed0dbbb-2500-5957-b946-27d34bce9614', 'El encargado', 'Most buildings have an **encargado** who lives in, keeps it clean and takes in your packages. He knows everything that happens on every **piso**.', 'draft'),
  ('d8622385-5e4a-5495-9a55-b9f96b8c8aeb', '9ed0dbbb-2500-5957-b946-27d34bce9614', 'Planta baja', 'The ground floor is **la planta baja**; the one above it is *el primer piso*. Ringing from the street, you press the **timbre** with the floor and the letter: *4° B*.', 'draft'),
  ('73641093-beea-5f10-a08c-70c533d5f13b', '2a79010a-154d-5c32-ade0-ded3031c92e1', 'Menos cuarto', 'After half past, porteños count down to the next hour: **las ocho menos cuarto** is *7:45*, *las nueve menos diez* is *8:50*. On the hour exactly: **a las diez en punto**.', 'draft'),
  ('7782b8c8-3797-5039-896a-6aeac6b65951', '2a79010a-154d-5c32-ade0-ded3031c92e1', 'Desde las nueve hasta las seis', 'Opening hours are **desde** … **hasta**: *abre desde las nueve hasta las seis*. At twelve it''s **el mediodía** — and plenty of small shops **cierran** for a while right after it.', 'draft'),
  ('a3090b0a-0804-5173-a69d-b576f39b56f7', '8b9e9239-cc97-56ec-b263-606f0591c15b', 'A la derecha, a la izquierda', 'Left and right both take **a la**: **a la derecha**, **a la izquierda**. Put them after the place: *la farmacia está a la derecha del banco*.', 'draft'),
  ('157ca385-f181-5461-aaa5-dc24d494945a', '8b9e9239-cc97-56ec-b263-606f0591c15b', 'A la vuelta', '**A la vuelta** is *around the corner* — on the next street, not this one. **En la otra vereda** is *across the street*: *la panadería está en la otra vereda*.', 'draft'),
  ('eeed7cdf-3b8e-5dff-9e77-07cb41136b16', '8b9e9239-cc97-56ec-b263-606f0591c15b', 'The shops of a barrio', 'Every shop is its product plus **-ería**: *carne* → **carnicería**, *pan* → *panadería*. The **almacén** is the old corner shop that sells a bit of everything.', 'draft'),
  ('8a7d639c-a995-52d0-bd64-ba1a51091f2d', 'ba4a74c0-9dbd-5b9f-9f9c-e2c7ec49c749', 'Prefiero, preferís', 'Same trick as *quiero, querés*: the stem stretches in most forms — **prefiero**, **vuelvo**, **empieza** — but vos keeps it short: **preferís**, **volvés**, **empezás**.', 'draft'),
  ('eb18db97-cf2e-5687-a5df-2e4b175166b1', 'ba4a74c0-9dbd-5b9f-9f9c-e2c7ec49c749', '¿A qué hora empieza?', 'For a film, a show or a party, ask **¿a qué hora empieza?** Porteños go out late: a **fiesta** that *empieza a las doce* is a normal Saturday, and you **volvés** at six.', 'draft'),
  ('adfbd620-4b64-5dad-b0f8-fbcbaea66bb8', 'b31e1b41-4887-5a80-bbf5-d83e7f8d4fac', 'When, where, who''s in', 'Making a plan takes three questions: **¿a qué hora?**, **¿dónde?** and **¿querés?** *Nos vemos a las nueve menos cuarto enfrente del cine, ¿podés?*', 'draft'),
  ('d36c5294-e7b3-5ec8-b07e-3985b2a03a0c', 'b31e1b41-4887-5a80-bbf5-d83e7f8d4fac', 'Vos keeps it short', 'Every stretchy verb stays short with vos: **querés, podés, preferís, volvés**. If you hear *quieres* or *puedes*, that''s not Buenos Aires.', 'draft'),
  ('6609448d-efbb-519e-83dd-ecfee61b264c', '714e2bff-95e1-5d03-96ef-a779c76932b7', 'Directions are commands', 'Same recipe as *mirá* and *vení*: drop the *-r*, stress the end. **Doblá**, **cruzá**, **caminá**, **preguntá**. *Seguir* says *sigo*, but its command is regular: **seguí**.', 'draft'),
  ('5c1d67c6-5ad4-5b4f-bfce-ca368b14bf65', '714e2bff-95e1-5d03-96ef-a779c76932b7', 'Bajate, subite', 'On the bondi, getting on and off carries a *te* on the end: **subite** (get on), **bajate en la esquina** (get off at the corner). And **pará** stops anyone: *¡pará, pará!*', 'draft'),
  ('6b5e4dd1-32f1-5210-99e2-f0e00d40b4cb', '714e2bff-95e1-5d03-96ef-a779c76932b7', '¿A qué altura?', 'Porteños give an address as street and number — *Corrientes 1500* — and ask **¿a qué altura?** to know which block. The number goes up about a hundred every **cuadra**.', 'draft'),
  ('790b188b-f364-5b46-be9e-6e1fe3996e4a', 'b3f216c5-d105-552a-b3f1-254cee4747b1', 'Colours that don''t change', '*Amarillo* works like *rojo*: **amarilla, amarillos**. But **gris** and **marrón** only add *-es* for more than one: *un buzo gris*, *unas zapatillas grises*.', 'draft'),
  ('1f894de9-91ad-51fe-8542-9bab3593187c', 'b3f216c5-d105-552a-b3f1-254cee4747b1', '¿Qué es eso?', 'When you don''t know the word — or don''t need it — use **esto** (this thing here) and **eso** (that thing there). They never change: *¿Cuánto sale eso?*', 'draft'),
  ('834d42dc-2aa8-5ffa-b50d-ecbf01e319e5', 'b3f216c5-d105-552a-b3f1-254cee4747b1', 'Talle', 'Your clothing size is your **talle**: *¿Qué talle sos?* Argentine sizes run small, so ask to try it on before you pay.', 'draft'),
  ('cb4863d6-1af9-5447-a5f5-2a898c771f96', '5f7fde3f-e53e-535b-a6d1-271ba38c2576', '¿Me cobrás?', 'To pay, you don''t say *I want to pay*: you ask them to charge you — **¿me cobrás?** The change they give back is **el vuelto**; small coins and notes are **cambio**.', 'draft'),
  ('34a36300-3e54-5655-a581-5b7afec4a3c2', '5f7fde3f-e53e-535b-a6d1-271ba38c2576', 'Thousands', 'Prices in Argentina run to thousands: **dos mil**, **quince mil**, **cien mil** — *mil* never takes an *s*. A **millón** does: *dos millones*.', 'draft'),
  ('034163a3-73bc-5439-b125-61d34a1ecfb1', 'b5b78e79-835c-570f-ad79-3070ec337571', 'Esa campera, ese buzo', 'The word for *that* matches the thing: **esa campera**, **ese buzo**, **esas zapatillas**. When you point without naming it, **eso** never changes.', 'draft'),
  ('a9c6a548-7adb-5fe2-ab0e-9c5cc53eefa8', 'b5b78e79-835c-570f-ad79-3070ec337571', 'Commands in the shop', 'The vos command is everywhere in a shop: **mirá**, **fijate**, **esperame**, **tomá** — *tomá, tu vuelto*. Drop the *-r*, stress the end.', 'draft'),
  ('63f1c92c-7c0e-5bd6-a215-09afc0414392', '86e40753-5eaa-5ab6-b540-6a6f01c756b5', 'Me despierto, se despiertan', 'Waking up and getting up are two verbs: **me despierto** at seven, **me levanto** at seven-thirty. The little word follows the person: *me, te, se, nos* — and **se** again for *ellos*: **se levantan**, **se acuestan**.', 'draft'),
  ('edcf0fa6-099e-5614-a334-06948ab92092', '86e40753-5eaa-5ab6-b540-6a6f01c756b5', 'Me voy', '*Voy* is where you''re going; **me voy** is that you''re leaving: *me voy a las ocho*. *¿Te vas?* — *Sí, me voy.*', 'draft'),
  ('c46ddd6d-9641-5450-a73f-0dad03d20e44', '86e40753-5eaa-5ab6-b540-6a6f01c756b5', 'La siesta', '**Dormir** stretches like *poder*: **duermo**, but **dormís**. And **la siesta** after lunch is still sacred in much of the country — less so in Buenos Aires.', 'draft'),
  ('c8ff9883-4beb-5661-a14b-579e206e4463', '1638cb53-0a6f-5b5b-a432-fa433a73461e', 'Le gusta, nos gusta, les gusta', 'The verb stays the same; only the little word changes with the person: **me** gusta, **te** gusta, **le** gusta, **nos** gusta, **les** gusta. *A mi hermano le gusta nadar.*', 'draft'),
  ('8f9901dd-7c3a-5f92-913a-3fbbf53ee713', '1638cb53-0a6f-5b5b-a432-fa433a73461e', 'Me interesa', '**Interesar** works exactly like *gustar*: **me interesa el tenis**, **no me interesan los deportes**. **Odiar** doesn''t — it''s a normal verb: **odio madrugar**.', 'draft'),
  ('e01853b2-a9b3-54dc-99d3-0a41ce6b109a', 'bd710c94-4059-585f-a6ad-448e21cec02f', 'Mucho calor, mucha humedad', 'With weather, *very* is **mucho**, not *muy*, because *calor* and *frío* are nouns: **hace mucho calor**, **hay mucha humedad** — *mucha* because *humedad* is feminine.', 'draft'),
  ('f30ace7e-6e7e-53a8-8879-9f8494a8c3ef', 'bd710c94-4059-585f-a6ad-448e21cec02f', 'Months and degrees', 'Months have no capital: **en marzo**, **en diciembre**. Temperature is **grados**: *hace treinta y cinco grados* is a normal January day, and porteños blame everything on **la humedad**.', 'draft'),
  ('ede1aee4-ba62-5a1d-ab4d-04909af86d9e', 'b10158d0-15cf-5719-a422-9a15ca8305ff', 'Me levanto, me gusta', 'Two *me*s that work differently. **Me levanto**: *I* get up. **Me gusta**: it pleases *me* — so **me gustan** when the thing is plural.', 'draft'),
  ('d64f5194-974a-5684-aa6f-fe1fca857c8b', 'b10158d0-15cf-5719-a422-9a15ca8305ff', 'Hace, hay, está', 'Weather takes three verbs: **hace** calor, **hay** humedad, **está** nublado — and **llueve** on its own.', 'draft'),
  ('3983d478-c583-5091-bd8f-2bdf626f07f0', '43405ea2-1cec-5d90-bb61-d884ee8359bb', '-ando, -iendo', 'Right now: **estar** + the verb in *-ando* (for -ar verbs) or *-iendo* (for -er and -ir): *estoy cocin**ando***, *está escrib**iendo***. Two to learn by ear: **leyendo**, **durmiendo**.', 'draft'),
  ('9bbe1671-a5b2-58f1-8923-ddd7c5e66973', '43405ea2-1cec-5d90-bb61-d884ee8359bb', 'El cumple', 'Nobody says *cumpleaños* in a hurry: it''s **el cumple**. *El sábado vamos a hacer el cumple de Juan* — and **pasado mañana** is *the day after tomorrow*.', 'draft'),
  ('6c3ed2e0-fd86-5836-9b3c-075d57051a2b', '6575b91f-226c-5859-990c-345608df8e59', 'Nosotros and ellos', 'The past has an ending for everyone: **cenamos** (we had dinner), **llegaron** (they arrived, you all arrived). *-amos* looks like the present — **anoche cenamos** tells you when — and *-aron* is only ever the past.', 'draft'),
  ('9e653eb3-9104-5de6-8e34-10e35edccdc3', '6575b91f-226c-5859-990c-345608df8e59', 'Pagué, llegué', 'In *yo* the spelling bends to keep the sound: *pagar* → **pagué**, *llegar* → **llegué**. At the end of the night someone says **pago yo** — and the next day, **ayer pagué yo, hoy te toca a vos**.', 'draft'),
  ('ef4a4d49-d272-5947-9455-4f31401357f9', 'de1b383d-ef2a-56ab-b9e0-5c76ea55a644', 'Your story in -í, -iste, -ió', '**Nací**, **viví**, **volví** — the -er and -ir past you already know tells a whole life. For *ellos* it ends in **-ieron**: *mis viejos **vivieron** en Brasil*, ***salieron** temprano*.', 'draft'),
  ('157adea8-7784-5a12-af4a-5816d2f99388', 'de1b383d-ef2a-56ab-b9e0-5c76ea55a644', '¿Dónde naciste?', 'It comes right after *¿de dónde sos?*: **¿Dónde naciste?** — **Nací en Rosario, pero vivo acá.** And when you didn''t catch what someone said, it''s the past too: **perdón, no entendí**.', 'draft'),
  ('8bf9ee78-2a14-5064-b2e2-5303c283c5b4', '470a7647-d7f6-51fe-aa77-1f96cda7ef58', 'Estuvimos, tuvimos', '*Estar* and *tener* keep their odd stem for everyone: **estuvimos**, **estuvieron**; **tuvimos**, **tuvieron**. And a night, a party or a gig **estuvo** good or bad: **¿Cómo estuvo el recital? Estuvo increíble.**', 'draft'),
  ('fc227694-2082-50f7-b840-d1a18ed3162f', '470a7647-d7f6-51fe-aa77-1f96cda7ef58', 'La pasé bárbaro', 'A porteño doesn''t *have fun*, he **la pasa**: **la pasé bárbaro**, **la pasamos re bien**, **¿la pasaste bien?** The *la* never changes and never means anything — it''s just part of the phrase.', 'draft'),
  ('22eec0b9-282c-5b1e-a1da-ffdac83fdbc5', 'c2f6a80b-fb62-5345-93bf-172376f29688', 'One past for everyone', '**Cené, cenaste, cenó, cenamos, cenaron**; **viví, viviste, vivió, vivimos, vivieron**. Two sets of endings, and every regular verb takes one of them.', 'draft'),
  ('9f2c679d-71d9-54c9-8c34-e6bc1cf22a1b', 'c2f6a80b-fb62-5345-93bf-172376f29688', 'We: now or then?', '**Cenamos** and **salimos** are both *today* and *yesterday*. A porteño never gets lost: **anoche**, **ayer**, **el sábado** — the time word does the work.', 'draft'),
  ('38bd929f-9d15-5f91-9679-cd20e1ac26fb', '59418fb1-6973-57d9-a75d-e4e21f004b8f', 'Traje, quise', 'Two more with their own stem and no accent: **traje, trajiste, trajo** (brought) and **quise, quisiste, quiso** (wanted, tried). **Quise venir, pero no pude** — *I wanted to come but couldn''t* — is the porteño apology for a missed asado.', 'draft'),
  ('b244722c-b0b4-5104-ac1c-9166a50e0dd0', '59418fb1-6973-57d9-a75d-e4e21f004b8f', 'Vinieron, hicieron, dijeron', 'For *ellos* the irregular ones end in **-ieron**: **vinieron**, **hicieron**, **pudieron** — and after a *j* just **-eron**: **dijeron**, **trajeron**. **No vino nadie** — *nobody came*: after the verb, *nadie* still needs the *no*.', 'draft'),
  ('01b9b81a-ee56-5eb2-b734-d0e5fef01d68', 'fa694c27-06c1-5cf3-b5c7-d369f495abbf', 'The most: el más … de', 'Put *el* or *la* in front of *más* and you have the top of the list: **el barrio más tranquilo**, **la calle más ruidosa**. The group comes after **de**, not *in*: **el mejor del mundo**, **la más linda de la ciudad**.', 'draft'),
  ('56a5e066-372b-51d5-87a3-d9b7c8977270', 'fa694c27-06c1-5cf3-b5c7-d369f495abbf', 'Lejísimos', '*-ísimo* works on almost anything: **baratísimo**, **lindísima**, and even on *lejos*: **queda lejísimos** — *it''s miles away*. A porteño uses it far more than *muy*.', 'draft'),
  ('1ef9dc5b-0a2c-51f6-8072-8665cac9cd15', '8a2211a4-bc2b-5df6-bfd3-ed761ce08c9a', 'Me dolió', '*Doler* has a past too, and it stays glued to its *me*: **me dolió la cabeza** — *my head hurt*, **¿te dolió?**, **a mi viejo le dolió la rodilla**. Two things hurting, two endings, just like *me duelen*: **me dolieron los ojos**.', 'draft'),
  ('be9c362e-6f84-5899-8e49-6046ba3c3cf3', '8a2211a4-bc2b-5df6-bfd3-ed761ce08c9a', 'At the farmacia', 'Half the time the pharmacist is your doctor: **estoy resfriado**, **tengo alergia**, **me duele el oído** — and you leave with **una curita** or **unas pastillas**.', 'draft'),
  ('ec6ca301-123b-5fdb-a569-f06e177afee5', '06235159-d29d-5425-954f-4a61a73fe1f4', 'Vino, trajo, quiso', 'The irregular past keeps one stem for every person: **vine, viniste, vino, vinimos, vinieron**. Learn the *yo* — **traje**, **quise**, **pude** — and the rest follows.', 'draft'),
  ('1a704bdb-e697-56e1-ac63-9b8b82d0aa54', '06235159-d29d-5425-954f-4a61a73fe1f4', 'The best, the worst', '**El mejor** and **el peor** need no *más*: **el mejor asado del barrio**, **el peor día de mi vida**. Everything else takes *el más*: **el más tranquilo**, **la más difícil**.', 'draft'),
  ('7fd0a81b-3973-5a9c-8a43-62a122aa7461', '77e7e28f-74ef-5a8f-b15f-5a5589d48684', 'Lo vi, la mandé', 'In the past the pronoun sits in the same place, before the verb: **¿Viste el mensaje? Sí, lo vi.** **¿Recibiste mi mail? No, no lo recibí.** *Le* and *les* too: **le di la llave**, **les mandé las fotos**.', 'draft'),
  ('310543d9-824f-5448-aa9c-02802ebdb8dd', '77e7e28f-74ef-5a8f-b15f-5a5589d48684', 'Mostrame', 'On a command it goes on the end, as before: **mostrame las fotos**, **buscame a las ocho**. The stress doesn''t move: *MOS-tra-me*, *BUS-ca-me*.', 'draft'),
  ('acd8e9ee-2a6d-5a93-a00c-59315ddfc1b2', '51c29d65-4514-5131-bc0a-f7a48158b1db', 'Me tocó', 'The turn has a past: **ayer me tocó a mí, hoy te toca a vos**. **¿A quién le tocó?** And the chores are plain -ar verbs: **lavé, limpiaste, ordenó** — only *sacar* bends in *yo*: **saqué**.', 'draft'),
  ('aff1bd39-fd35-5a50-a00c-5f4e71e503d4', '51c29d65-4514-5131-bc0a-f7a48158b1db', 'El lavarropas', 'Clothes go in the **lavarropas**; to **planchar** them is optional for most porteños. The floor: **barrer** with the **escoba**, then a wet **trapo** — *el trapo de piso*.', 'draft'),
  ('bd5cb236-10da-572d-bfd4-d7b1bf5ec6d4', '76cdfd4e-984e-51cf-b109-bac7fe371aa2', 'The holiday is a we', 'Holidays are told in *nosotros*: **alquilamos**, **nadamos**, **paseamos**, **sacamos mil fotos**. And the weather that ruined it: **llovió toda la semana**.', 'draft'),
  ('3e0c5f85-ecd5-5ae1-8b4e-81aea77a6ddc', '76cdfd4e-984e-51cf-b109-bac7fe371aa2', 'La costa, las sierras', 'For a porteño **la costa** is the Atlantic beaches south of the city, where you rent a **carpa** — a beach tent by the day. The other classic is **las sierras** of Córdoba: a **río** instead of the sea.', 'draft'),
  ('41d6e773-f273-597c-b1d9-b84748b48c24', '4147926b-4f4c-5a4e-83c2-d3a82772d8e7', 'Lo mandé, la vi', 'The pronoun works the same in the past: **¿Las fotos? Ya las mandé.** **¿El video? Lo vi anoche.** **¿A Sofi? La llamé ayer.** Before the verb, always — only a command takes it on the end.', 'draft'),
  ('a160a37a-11e9-53de-a76b-fce48eb65bc8', '4147926b-4f4c-5a4e-83c2-d3a82772d8e7', 'Me tocó, llovió', 'Some verbs only ever show up in the *it* form: **me tocó**, **me dolió**, **llovió**. Learn them as the whole thing.', 'draft'),
  ('1e7c5ba0-2e13-56f5-a9d6-1ac8f4aa5660', '89b3ea96-dbbd-5f0a-8651-0308bd27297d', 'Así que, por eso', 'The story moves on with **así que** — *so* — and explains itself with **por eso** — *that''s why*: **Perdí el bondi, así que llegué tarde.** **Llovió; por eso no fui.**', 'draft'),
  ('eb59a72a-6a28-5738-a51c-17fb712b380a', '89b3ea96-dbbd-5f0a-8651-0308bd27297d', '¡Menos mal!', 'When the story ends well, the listener says **¡menos mal!** — *thank goodness!* — and **¡por fin!** when it took forever. **¿En serio?** keeps you talking: *really?*', 'draft'),
  ('336a59db-57e8-5592-a23c-c6ed5f4ff51a', 'dae6ceff-5881-5f5c-a218-3a6317889caf', 'They and we', 'For *ellos* and *ustedes* the imperfect just adds an **-n**: **eran**, **tenían**, **vivían**, **iban**. For *nosotros* it''s **-íamos**: **teníamos**, **vivíamos**. *Mis abuelos vivían en el campo y nosotros íbamos en enero.*', 'draft'),
  ('a54ef42c-739f-54d1-8ea1-e7c82fed10f9', 'dae6ceff-5881-5f5c-a218-3a6317889caf', 'Quería, podía', 'Wanting and being able are states, so back then they take the imperfect: **De chico quería ser maestro** — *as a kid I wanted to be a teacher*; **no podía salir solo** — *I wasn''t allowed out on my own*. **En esa época** — *back then* — sets the whole scene.', 'draft'),
  ('a4adbfa9-faf6-51e4-b4d1-cd956aff9126', 'dae6ceff-5881-5f5c-a218-3a6317889caf', 'La primaria', 'School here is **la primaria** (six to twelve) and then **la secundaria**. In a state school everyone wears a white **guardapolvo** over their clothes, and the best part of the day is the **recreo**.', 'draft'),
  ('9ec733ad-4e25-5b19-b479-b8f53e3f3e70', '018363b6-528d-5464-8f6a-575ede7fb4d4', 'Me gustaba, me encantaba', '*Gustar* and *encantar* go back like any -ar verb: **me gustaba**, **me encantaba** — *I liked*, *I loved*. Several things take an **-n**: **me gustaban los dibujitos**. *Ellos* too: **los pibes jugaban a la pelota en la vereda**.', 'draft'),
  ('d89da798-e89a-57e0-8335-8034a0f0ffe4', '018363b6-528d-5464-8f6a-575ede7fb4d4', 'Ver keeps its e', 'Only three verbs break the pattern: *ser* (**era**), *ir* (**iba**) and *ver*, which keeps its *e*: **veía**, **veías**. Set **antes** against **ahora** and the imperfect does the rest: *antes veía dibujitos, ahora veo series.*', 'draft'),
  ('5ddf4028-735b-5fe9-bb76-452650143280', 'a6cc868d-fbe9-5af4-94d5-1396172806b6', 'Right in the middle', '**Estaba** + *-ando, -iendo* puts you in the middle of something: **estaba durmiendo**, **estábamos cocinando**. Then the preterite cuts in: **cuando sonó el celu**, **cuando entró Juan**.', 'draft'),
  ('c6bd0210-8827-5e7e-99e5-ec0492090bc7', 'a6cc868d-fbe9-5af4-94d5-1396172806b6', 'Era la una, eran las tres', 'The time in a story is always imperfect: **era la una**, **eran las tres**. The scene keeps going — **no había luz**, **hacía un calor bárbaro** — until something happens: **empezó a llover** and **llegué mojado**.', 'draft'),
  ('b82e3b85-1c79-5d6c-9b43-76d0bec86a2f', '3daf878e-35b0-55b7-8541-2ed16c89f822', 'Everyone, back then', '**Era, eras, éramos, eran**; **tenía, tenías, teníamos, tenían**; **jugaba, jugabas, jugábamos, jugaban**. One set of endings for *-ar*, one for *-er* and *-ir*, and *yo* and *él* always share.', 'draft'),
  ('592b7207-8f86-5875-b8bb-9d2027eff517', '3daf878e-35b0-55b7-8541-2ed16c89f822', 'The scene, then the event', '**Estaba en la ducha** — the scene; **cuando sonó el timbre** — the event. If you could add *was …-ing* or *used to* in English, it''s the imperfect; if it happened once and ended, it''s the preterite.', 'draft'),
  ('020df5a2-fd52-5796-ab77-690cad166bff', '42aaa1de-ab7b-5276-9032-91bff95ed818', 'Pide, pidieron', 'The *e* of *pedir* turns into *i* in **pido**, **pide**, and in the past for *él* and *ellos*: **pidió**, **pidieron**. *Nosotros* keeps its *e* — and **pedimos** is both *we order* and *we ordered*: **anoche pedimos empanadas**.', 'draft'),
  ('f63133a6-64bd-5ac4-8b7d-bdbc4f0e61d8', '42aaa1de-ab7b-5276-9032-91bff95ed818', 'La pizzería', 'Porteño pizza is its own religion: **una grande de muzza**, eaten standing up by the **porción**, or **mitad y mitad** for the undecided. At a parrilla, start with a **provoleta** and a **choripán**. **Reservé para las diez** — dinner starts late.', 'draft'),
  ('3c539a2a-3a6b-501b-8da7-a0d344dac0f0', '08e5edec-37ff-59c8-942b-b0fcd4fe251b', '¿Por qué no me avisaste?', '*Avisar* in the past is plain -ar: **avisé**, **avisaste**, **avisó**. The text at eight-forty: **¿Dónde estás? Te esperaba a las ocho.** And the answer, always: **ya salgo**.', 'draft'),
  ('46a3767c-afbc-5de8-b6ed-18d09ef114cb', '08e5edec-37ff-59c8-942b-b0fcd4fe251b', 'Tardar', '**Tardar** is *to take (time)*: **¿cuánto tardás?** — *how long will you be?*; **el bondi tardó una hora**. Porteños are rarely **puntuales**, so *tipo ocho* already has the delay built in.', 'draft'),
  ('32fcb37a-4aa0-51cd-81ec-b1e0bd63b3f5', '9c4215a1-3276-5ea1-9982-970e15e17072', 'En lo de', '**En lo de mi abuela** — *at my grandma''s (place)*; **vamos a lo de Juan** — *let''s go to Juan''s*. Sunday lunch there meant **ravioles con tuco**, and a **sobremesa** that ran until the *mate* came out.', 'draft'),
  ('a080f44e-07bb-51f5-922b-1fe5cf6548e6', '9c4215a1-3276-5ea1-9982-970e15e17072', 'What the family always did', 'Traditions told from back then are imperfect: **nos juntábamos**, **festejábamos**, **armábamos el arbolito** on the 8th of December. **Tenemos la costumbre de…** — *we have the custom of…* — brings them into the present.', 'draft'),
  ('9ca4eeb2-2fba-5a4a-bc62-2e939d24aea0', '3f5edc68-b41a-525a-8c80-1dd31a54500b', 'Pedimos, pidieron', '**Pedimos** keeps the *e*; **pidió** and **pidieron** turn it into *i*. **¿Qué pidieron?** — **Una grande de muzza y dos porciones de provoleta**... and someone always asks **¿es picante?**', 'draft'),
  ('057623fc-a147-5b44-8c1e-bb25b38b1c59', '3f5edc68-b41a-525a-8c80-1dd31a54500b', 'What we always did', 'Habits from back then are imperfect: **los domingos nos juntábamos**, **festejábamos Reyes**. One night that went wrong is preterite: **el bondi tardó una hora y no te avisé**.', 'draft'),
  ('964d8d97-0008-5be6-9c6c-5b9aeb6147f7', 'da8af0c4-83d8-570d-a76d-ba9f8a8d4161', 'Me olvidé de…', '**Me olvidé de su cumple** — *I forgot his birthday*. Like *me enojé* it keeps its pronoun, and takes **de** before what you forgot. Same shape: **me aburrí**, **me calmé**, **me enamoré** — of Buenos Aires, of course.', 'draft'),
  ('80ff4fcc-3b03-5cec-bc11-61a64e3a76c3', 'da8af0c4-83d8-570d-a76d-ba9f8a8d4161', 'Estaba or me puse?', '**Estaba nervioso** is how you were; **me puse nervioso** is the moment it hit you. **Me sentí re mal** — *I felt awful* — is one moment too. For *us*: **nos pusimos contentos**; and **me puse colorado** is *I blushed*.', 'draft'),
  ('2687eaa7-c8c7-574a-ac6c-804da3aa671d', 'e550d21d-40fe-5eaa-a4c9-c6b22660639b', 'Voy a tener que', '**Tengo que** is *I have to*; put **voy a** in front for later: **voy a tener que laburar** — *I''m going to have to work*. It works for everyone: **vas a tener que**, **vamos a tener que**.', 'draft'),
  ('866c54a2-1f90-5dd1-9448-907edaf16201', 'e550d21d-40fe-5eaa-a4c9-c6b22660639b', 'Some day, soon', '**Pronto** — *soon*; **a fin de año** — *at the end of the year*; **algún día** — *some day*. **¿Qué piensan hacer?** — **Pensamos juntar plata para viajar.** And the classic January promise: **me anoté en el gimnasio**.', 'draft'),
  ('c7afa899-216a-57fc-be0d-9bbb58ad2055', 'e4a7439f-a626-5e82-abaf-06bc10e30be8', 'Before and after the move', 'The old place is imperfect, the move is preterite: **antes alquilaba en el centro** — how it was; **me mudé a Palermo** — what happened. **Extrañaba** is missing that went on: *los primeros meses extrañaba mi barrio*.', 'draft'),
  ('dbad321f-4d32-5c2e-9458-5dbbd5e6decf', 'e4a7439f-a626-5e82-abaf-06bc10e30be8', 'The move itself', 'You rent through an **inmobiliaria** or straight from the **dueño**, and pay the **alquiler** every month. A **flete** takes the **muebles** and the **colchón**, and before unpacking, **pintamos**. The neighbours say it of you: **se mudaron**.', 'draft'),
  ('dc0add60-c2f1-5dee-a7f9-64d45cc91661', 'ab5b06cb-0463-5884-9c6f-bc21a5b345d8', 'Three times in one breath', '**Antes alquilaba** — how it was; **me mudé** — what happened; **voy a pintar** — what''s next. A porteño story moves through all three without stopping.', 'draft'),
  ('dff327fb-93ad-5ef0-b87d-b0b8f5748f57', 'ab5b06cb-0463-5884-9c6f-bc21a5b345d8', 'Me sentí, me olvidé', 'The moment a feeling hit, or a thing slipped your mind, is preterite with its pronoun: **me sentí re mal**, **me olvidé de avisarte**, **nos pusimos contentos**.', 'draft'),
  ('d68950b1-3362-55bb-81f3-e4f576a91d8e', 'd738540e-3203-5d07-9f0f-01184e8346b0', 'Acordarse de', 'What you remember takes **de**: **mi abuela se acuerda de todo**; **nos acordamos de esa época**. **No me acordaba** — *I''d forgotten* — is imperfect: the not-remembering went on until someone brought it up.', 'draft'),
  ('086d52a0-b9ba-519c-bd38-ddf31a31c648', 'd738540e-3203-5d07-9f0f-01184e8346b0', 'Every porteño childhood', 'The **calesita** in the plaza, **golosinas** from the kiosco, a **barrilete** on a windy day, and the **álbum** of **figuritas** you filled for every **Mundial** — **lo llenábamos entre todos**.', 'draft'),
  ('795eda4c-1b7a-5a23-97b2-aac4263f8f38', '1bb1664d-4c74-5dfd-9001-ae7f0fd897f8', 'More favours, same question', 'Still a plain question in the present with **me** in front: **¿me acompañás?** — *will you come with me?*; **¿me llevás?** — *can you give me a lift?*; **¿me regás las plantas?** — *can you water my plants?* Asking for two of you? Swap *me* for **nos**: **¿nos sacás una foto?**', 'draft'),
  ('db277486-1a0c-5764-957e-645579b22f1a', '1bb1664d-4c74-5dfd-9001-ae7f0fd897f8', 'Prendé, apagá', 'Between friends a bare vos command is polite enough: **prendé la luz**, **apagá la tele**. With a pronoun it joins the end, like *ayudame*: **acompañame**, **llevame**.', 'draft'),
  ('cc550005-16ab-5bc9-82ad-d31a38137a15', '1bb1664d-4c74-5dfd-9001-ae7f0fd897f8', 'Un toque, de paso', '**Un toque** is *a sec, a bit*: **esperame un toque**. **De paso** is *while you''re at it*: **de paso, ¿me comprás pan?**', 'draft'),
  ('84966d7c-a1bb-545e-b107-22c27cd2689c', 'c0772c39-c5d2-5576-a853-6b67b734fca4', 'Tenés que, tiene que, tienen que', '**Tener que** changes with the person: **tenés que firmar** — *you have to sign*; **tiene que venir ella** — *she has to come*; **tienen que esperar** — *they have to wait*. **Hay que** stays the same for everyone: **hay que hacer la fila**.', 'draft'),
  ('d621bc2e-5355-512b-b832-644c850667ef', 'c0772c39-c5d2-5576-a853-6b67b734fca4', 'Hace falta', 'Drop the *no* and **hace falta** says what''s needed: **hace falta una fotocopia**. **¿Es obligatorio?** — *is it required?* — is the question every porteño asks at the counter.', 'draft'),
  ('a1d65681-cd3c-511c-ba20-f092864449fc', 'c0772c39-c5d2-5576-a853-6b67b734fca4', 'Sacar turno', 'Nothing happens in Buenos Aires without **sacar turno** — *booking an appointment*, almost always **por internet**. Then they **piden** things: **te piden una fotocopia**, **completá esto**.', 'draft'),
  ('3d839f10-580b-59a4-ae50-d6b91d794967', '3ea35fe7-87c2-5bc5-a138-ed2124298174', 'Gramos, litros', 'At the fiambrería you buy by the **gramo**: **cien gramos de jamón**, cut in **fetas** — *slices*. Milk comes by the **litro**. At the **feria**, the street market, it''s still the kilo: **medio kilo de frutillas**.', 'draft'),
  ('bbf8899a-9326-5028-a470-f2f170b9aab4', '3ea35fe7-87c2-5bc5-a138-ed2124298174', '¿Algo más?', 'The vendor always asks **¿algo más?** — *anything else?* You answer **nada más, gracias**, or keep going: **y un kilo de naranjas**.', 'draft'),
  ('fbd2133a-6859-5ead-9926-d782de4ed43e', '3ea35fe7-87c2-5bc5-a138-ed2124298174', 'Palta, frutilla', 'Buenos Aires has its own names: **palta** is avocado, **frutilla** is strawberry. Before you buy, ask **¿están maduras?** — *are they ripe?*', 'draft'),
  ('4492bbdb-8376-566f-af0c-068e10d3456b', '3714140a-6ac1-56cd-a67b-a1c55a28970b', 'Me, nos', '**¿Me acompañás?** asks for you; **¿nos sacás una foto?** asks for the two of you. It stays a question in the present — the tone does the asking.', 'draft'),
  ('75ad7c7e-3128-5ad9-82e3-98786bab822e', '3714140a-6ac1-56cd-a67b-a1c55a28970b', 'Tenés que, hace falta', '**Tenés que llevar una fotocopia** — someone has to; **hace falta una fotocopia** — something is needed; **no hace falta** — relax.', 'draft'),
  ('ecc14597-b02a-597b-bcbf-15a4de418dbf', '533e4252-0503-516b-9b3f-fcc7fad29ce1', 'Se apagó, se colgó', 'A machine that fails on its own takes **se** and the preterite, like **se cortó**: **se apagó** — *it switched off*; **se colgó** — *it froze*. Your friend **se quedó sin batería**? Now you know why she didn''t answer.', 'draft'),
  ('524511f8-c321-5aef-8f9b-ac4dc642abad', '533e4252-0503-516b-9b3f-fcc7fad29ce1', 'Sin señal, sin datos', '**No tengo señal** — *no signal*; **me quedé sin datos** — *I ran out of data*. **¿Tenés wifi?** is the first question at any bar.', 'draft'),
  ('6d7c070b-81b7-58f0-b526-20ee5f75be8d', '533e4252-0503-516b-9b3f-fcc7fad29ce1', 'Descargá, borrá', 'Tech orders are plain vos commands: **descargá la app**, **borrá las fotos**. What you already did is preterite: **la descargué**, **las borré**.', 'draft'),
  ('048d0617-122d-5211-9fbc-9f90ff0d21da', 'c7ff7e62-3fed-5f39-9dbd-9ed081d41b0c', 'Te lo presto', 'Same order as *te lo devuelvo* — person first, thing second — with new verbs: **te lo presto** — *I''ll lend it to you*; **te lo guardo** — *I''ll keep it for you*; **te lo muestro** — *I''ll show you*. The thing decides lo or la: *¿la bici?* **te la presto**.', 'draft'),
  ('54258bee-3191-56f9-842b-a737c8639654', 'c7ff7e62-3fed-5f39-9dbd-9ed081d41b0c', 'Se lo digo', 'To him or her, *le lo* turns into **se lo** again: **se lo digo mañana** — *I''ll tell her tomorrow*. If it''s **un secreto**: **no se lo digo a nadie, te lo prometo**.', 'draft'),
  ('eac9e910-201e-59a3-84b2-284e5bde2587', 'c7ff7e62-3fed-5f39-9dbd-9ed081d41b0c', 'Te lo paso', '**Te lo paso** is *I''ll send it to you* — a number, a photo, a file. And a loan comes back **sin falta** — *without fail*.', 'draft'),
  ('7b3f9683-3acc-59a1-ae9e-57f15db24ae0', 'd674935a-006a-5328-9d83-767165ba5c5a', 'Consiguió, renunció', 'The rest of the story is in the third person: **consiguió laburo** — *she got a job*; **renunció** — *he quit*. Nobody says *fue despedido*: it''s **lo echaron** — *they fired him* — and **me contrataron** — *they hired me*.', 'draft'),
  ('47214591-3206-5367-b7d5-e48af1764d9e', 'd674935a-006a-5328-9d83-767165ba5c5a', 'El aguinaldo', 'Twice a year, in June and December, the **aguinaldo** arrives — half a month''s extra pay. Ask about it at the *entrevista*, along with the **licencia** — *paid leave*.', 'draft'),
  ('3cc9772d-ff26-53c8-bc0e-75e10b409d89', 'd674935a-006a-5328-9d83-767165ba5c5a', 'El currículum', 'You send **el currículum**; they ask about your **experiencia** and tell you about the **puesto** — *the position*. **Pagan bien** — *they pay well* — is what everyone wants to hear.', 'draft'),
  ('38d97ecf-5b4f-55d9-ac27-d033c55bfdfd', '3aec2726-7aa4-581b-ab9a-b36725c94e5c', 'Se colgó, se apagó', 'A machine that fails on its own: **se** + preterite. **Se colgó la compu**, **se apagó el celu**, **se quedó sin batería**.', 'draft'),
  ('56d9af58-9350-5687-a52f-ca67ee2f2167', '3aec2726-7aa4-581b-ab9a-b36725c94e5c', 'Te lo, se lo', '**Te lo presto**, **te lo paso**, **se lo digo** — person first, thing second, and *le* becomes **se**.', 'draft'),
  ('59b9932a-0c66-512c-8ceb-29bf2dcbb476', '672335a3-bca5-50a9-a61e-0d2d70bcd3c2', 'Nos conocemos, se separaron', 'The *each other* verbs take **nos** for *we* and **se** for *they*: **nos conocemos del laburo** — *we know each other from work*; **nos escribimos** — *we write to each other*; **se separaron** — *they split up*; **siempre se pelean** — *they''re always fighting*.', 'draft'),
  ('5da4918a-949d-5581-912f-9ba1ea88bde3', '672335a3-bca5-50a9-a61e-0d2d70bcd3c2', 'Estamos de novios', 'Going steady is **estar de novios**. The one before is **mi ex**, and a porteño couple calls each other **amor**. When it ends badly: **me dejó** — *she left me* — or **me mintió** — *he lied to me*.', 'draft'),
  ('3e1aea26-3240-5b82-8f97-779422c5dfd4', '672335a3-bca5-50a9-a61e-0d2d70bcd3c2', 'Confiar en', '**Confío en vos** — *I trust you*. Trust always takes **en**.', 'draft'),
  ('ce15b13a-f188-5034-9617-6e20515212c2', '7799d9a3-02e3-5465-af9f-63e41620d4a7', 'Any verb can wonder', '**¿Qué hará?** — *I wonder what he''s up to*; **¿cuánto costará?** — *I wonder how much it is*; **vendrá más tarde** — *she''ll probably come later*. For a group: **estarán en el bar**, **serán las ocho**. To vos: **¿estarás cansado?** — *you must be tired*.', 'draft'),
  ('4bc58977-3301-5ff9-a185-6d0a353d4619', '7799d9a3-02e3-5465-af9f-63e41620d4a7', 'Hará, vendrá, podrá', 'The irregular ones squeeze the verb: *hacer* → **hará**, *venir* → **vendrá**, *poder* → **podrá**, *querer* → **querrá**. The ending is always **-á** for él and ella.', 'draft'),
  ('35d7e2d9-c8c8-578d-8daa-4feb050372e1', '7799d9a3-02e3-5465-af9f-63e41620d4a7', 'A lo mejor, supongo', 'The same guess in the present: **a lo mejor** — *maybe*; **supongo que sí** — *I suppose so*; **probablemente** — *probably*.', 'draft'),
  ('0f049b73-270c-5432-ac80-f9307754ce21', '1a2ffa4b-843c-5a51-a395-8402da85268f', 'Se siente', 'For someone else it''s **se siente**: **mi abuela no se siente bien**. Where it hurts is **dolor de** — **tengo dolor de cabeza** — *I''ve got a headache*.', 'draft'),
  ('edf96564-1272-5295-ac16-0720fe0808d9', '1a2ffa4b-843c-5a51-a395-8402da85268f', 'La prepaga', 'Besides the public hospital and the *obra social*, lots of porteños pay a **prepaga** — a private health plan. Once a year: a **chequeo**, an *análisis de* **sangre**, **la presión** and **la vacuna** for the **gripe**.', 'draft'),
  ('c2f0319f-90fd-58b6-9103-689c477c4991', '1a2ffa4b-843c-5a51-a395-8402da85268f', 'Reposo', 'The doctor''s favourite order: **tenés que hacer reposo** — *you need bed rest*. And a **jarabe** for the cough.', 'draft'),
  ('dc575731-6236-5ebb-ad27-018912b6ec65', '2dc6362b-360e-5e66-9d76-2c5e8662af80', 'Wonder, then guess', '**¿Qué le pasará?** wonders; **a lo mejor tiene gripe** guesses; **seguro que está cansado** bets. The future to wonder, the present to guess.', 'draft'),
  ('27b027db-cc99-5f61-aa67-c7e82f54e4e2', '2dc6362b-360e-5e66-9d76-2c5e8662af80', 'Nos, se', '**Nos conocemos**, **nos escribimos** — each other, us. **Se separaron**, **se pelean** — each other, them.', 'draft'),
  ('88b903ef-e0f8-5900-86f6-a2d183e36dce', '7a4e0aad-1065-50e6-bfec-a76b27e0ed9a', 'Me salvaste', 'Porteño thanks praise the person: **sos un genio**, **me salvaste** — *you saved me*. For a present: **¡qué detalle!** — *how thoughtful!* — or **¡te pasaste!** — *you shouldn''t have!*', 'draft'),
  ('c38f50d3-9623-5a55-a0db-04a1f20fe7a0', '7a4e0aad-1065-50e6-bfec-a76b27e0ed9a', 'Ni hablar', 'The answers: *de nada*, **ni hablar** — *don''t mention it* — or **lo hice de onda** — *I did it to be nice*. **Te agradezco** is warmer and a touch more formal: **te agradezco de verdad**.', 'draft'),
  ('f5455265-bb74-5249-8a14-6de9374128c9', '26521f59-c2c1-5009-ac6b-a7a2e20a4244', 'Necesito que, te pido que', '*Quiero que* has company: **necesito que me ayudes** — *I need you to help me*; **te pido que me esperes** — *please wait for me*. Whenever you want something from someone else, the verb after *que* goes subjunctive.', 'draft'),
  ('28a65e19-7a4f-501c-bed5-b872f18ca04c', '26521f59-c2c1-5009-ac6b-a7a2e20a4244', '-ar takes e, the rest take a', '**Ayudar → ayudes**, **esperar → esperes**, **mandar → mandes**, **avisar → avises**. *Poner* and *traer* keep the *g* of their *yo*: **pongo → pongas**, **traigo → traiga**. For *we*: **¿qué querés que hagamos?**', 'draft'),
  ('c877808b-888c-5895-a77e-cfe7de1b954b', '26521f59-c2c1-5009-ac6b-a7a2e20a4244', '¿Querés que te lleve?', 'The offer is the same pattern turned round: **¿querés que te lleve?** — *want a lift?*; **¿querés que traiga algo?** — the guest''s question before every *asado*. The *yo* form matches *él*: **lleve**, **traiga**, **ponga**.', 'draft'),
  ('13fcd638-5332-5252-85cd-fbcbb2a103dd', '1c051416-1a0d-564f-bb98-d523f047c49e', 'Que te mejores, que te diviertas', 'The pronoun comes along into the wish: **que te mejores** — *get well soon*; **que te diviertas** — *have fun*; **que lo disfrutes** — *enjoy it*. The same *que* + subjunctive as *que te vaya bien*.', 'draft'),
  ('95398e1c-e3f0-54d8-a109-adab96baa057', '1c051416-1a0d-564f-bb98-d523f047c49e', 'Ojalá, for all of us', '**Ojalá que ganemos** — *I hope we win* — *-ar* verbs swap to **e** for *we* too. For a present: **ojalá que te guste**. On a birthday: **¡feliz cumple, que cumplas muchos más!**', 'draft'),
  ('7d1e7009-9fe1-5c8d-a766-f7661c904b77', '1c051416-1a0d-564f-bb98-d523f047c49e', '¡Éxitos!', 'Before an interview or an exam porteños say **¡éxitos!** or **¡mucha suerte!** — and before a **parcial**, the university exam everyone dreads, both.', 'draft'),
  ('fd31828f-1076-5a16-bc3b-deb3ac0247e5', '54074d45-5916-5f81-b9e2-24977fe7b3b0', 'Cuando vuelvas', 'Plans hang on *cuando* + the subjunctive: **cuando vuelvas del viaje, nos vemos**; **cuando cobre, te invito**; **cuando empieces el laburo nuevo, contame**. The other half stays normal, or a command.', 'draft'),
  ('cc1eb54f-1d60-5e64-a9c3-fc54886b6765', '54074d45-5916-5f81-b9e2-24977fe7b3b0', 'Hasta que, apenas', '**Hasta que** — *until* — works the same way: **esperá hasta que te llame**. And the promise every porteño makes: **apenas sepa algo, te aviso** — *as soon as I know anything, I''ll tell you*.', 'draft'),
  ('b8dca299-9d43-5311-998f-70c610f3b72b', '54074d45-5916-5f81-b9e2-24977fe7b3b0', 'Cuando quieras', '**Cuando quieras** — *whenever you like* — leaves an invitation open. The question keeps the normal verb: **¿cuándo nos vemos?** The answer doesn''t: **cuando nos veamos, te cuento**.', 'draft'),
  ('e540a8ba-197a-50dd-9f1c-cf330b9d8d07', 'f93505a1-733d-5810-85e8-c9d6ead98847', 'Needing, asking, offering', '**Necesito que me ayudes**, **te pido que me esperes**, **¿querés que te lleve?** When the person after *que* is someone else, the verb goes subjunctive.', 'draft'),
  ('a2b395ff-2333-5d36-83a5-22f200388a41', 'f93505a1-733d-5810-85e8-c9d6ead98847', 'Wishes and not-yet', 'Wishes and the future share the form: **que te diviertas**, **ojalá que ganemos**, **cuando vuelvas**, **hasta que sepa algo**.', 'draft'),
  ('3f9a94db-3f58-5df2-a5ce-ffab9f03c71e', '5f67ad55-d966-5850-bf7c-244e5934bef7', 'Puede ser que', '**Puede ser** — *could be* — is the porteño *maybe*. Add *que* and the subjunctive follows: **puede ser que llueva**, **puede ser que estén cerrados**. **Es posible que** says the same, a little more carefully.', 'draft'),
  ('ebd96385-ca9b-589a-b0c0-208f0ecb6435', '5f67ad55-d966-5850-bf7c-244e5934bef7', 'No creo que, for them', 'For *they*, add **-n** to the form you know: **no creo que sean**, **no creo que estén**, **no creo que tengan**, **no creo que puedan**, **no creo que lleguen a tiempo**.', 'draft'),
  ('ec947d91-a8f3-55dd-9486-0b6d2e5c67cb', '5f67ad55-d966-5850-bf7c-244e5934bef7', 'Seguro, or not', 'Sure of it? The normal verb: **seguro que abren**, **creo que viene**. Doubt it, and the subjunctive: **no creo que abran hoy**. Even the forecast gets the treatment: **el pronóstico es de lluvia, pero no creo que llueva**.', 'draft'),
  ('2154bd17-d52c-5d24-9381-88a172c5d5eb', 'a52f663a-3fe2-5627-a250-4d1da0344831', 'No dejes, no saques', 'Every *don''t* grows from the *yo* of the present: *dejo* → **no dejes**, *cuento* → **no cuentes**. *Saco* gives **no saques**: the *c* turns *qu* to keep its sound.', 'draft'),
  ('7fd63f41-d782-569a-9458-af9206b8cc0e', 'a52f663a-3fe2-5627-a250-4d1da0344831', 'No te vayas, no te pongas así', 'With *-se* verbs the pronoun goes in front: *me voy* → **no te vayas**, *me puse nervioso* → **no te pongas nervioso**, *me enojé* → **no te enojes**. And the one every friend says: **no seas así**.', 'draft'),
  ('0e231c01-9ba1-54a5-bba5-14f03f21360d', 'd7a7375d-a258-5d7a-ae1c-44811ac8b58d', 'Te aconsejo que, conviene que', '**Te aconsejo que lleves efectivo** — *my advice: bring cash*. **Conviene que** and **es importante que** work like *te recomiendo que*: the advice lands in the subjunctive — **conviene que busques un depto cerca del subte**.', 'draft'),
  ('130803a8-884e-58f5-94ba-fbcb5e107882', 'd7a7375d-a258-5d7a-ae1c-44811ac8b58d', 'Advice for us', 'Advice to the group uses *we*: **es mejor que salgamos temprano**, **conviene que lo hagamos mañana**. *Salir* keeps its *g*: *salgo* → **salgamos**.', 'draft'),
  ('c25e8bf6-8957-58ac-8fc3-5770ed6b1bef', 'd7a7375d-a258-5d7a-ae1c-44811ac8b58d', '¡Cuidado!', '**¡Cuidado!** — *careful!* — and the tips that come with it: **no uses el celu en la calle**, **es mejor que cambies plata antes**, **te conviene reservar**.', 'draft'),
  ('867d2ad3-cf6a-54b5-b0fb-95b84517f45c', 'ea336760-e828-5137-b3c0-b527db54dc8f', 'They, and we', 'The subjunctive for *they* adds **-n** (**sean**, **estén**, **lleguen**); for *we*, **-mos** (**salgamos**, **hagamos**). The rule for when to use it doesn''t change.', 'draft'),
  ('b51fd619-259c-591e-b3ef-373e849c2d56', 'ea336760-e828-5137-b3c0-b527db54dc8f', 'Don''t, and better not', '**No dejes el celu ahí** is a warning; **es mejor que no lo dejes ahí** is advice. Both take the subjunctive.', 'draft'),
  ('5fb42aa7-4492-5e62-a9a8-5be34c1812ac', '166d8cfb-d95b-581c-aaa6-c1689dc3d072', 'Me preocupa que', '**Me preocupa que trabajes tanto** — *it worries me that you work so much*. *Preocupar* and *sorprender* work like *gustar* and *molestar*: **me preocupa**, **me sorprende**, and what follows *que* goes subjunctive.', 'draft'),
  ('74fa9b53-73dc-5c65-939e-c1ab90984cc7', '166d8cfb-d95b-581c-aaa6-c1689dc3d072', 'Qué suerte que', '**¡Qué suerte que vivas cerca!**, **¡qué bueno que estemos todos juntos!** Any *qué* + reaction + *que* brings the subjunctive. **Estemos** is *we*: the same form plus **-mos**.', 'draft'),
  ('bdfa6d19-b498-5304-90d9-5803762aa37f', '644dde8f-bffc-57c9-b92e-ec8b078e378c', '¿Qué significa?', 'Stuck on a word: **¿qué significa?** Too fast? **¿Me lo repetís?** or **repetime, más despacio**. Got it wrong? **Perdón, me confundí.**', 'draft'),
  ('6275c34f-71c5-56f0-8a46-1c252791699f', '644dde8f-bffc-57c9-b92e-ec8b078e378c', 'No sabía, me di cuenta', '**No sabía** — *I didn''t know* — is the imperfect: a state. **Me di cuenta** — *I realised* — is the moment it clicked. **¿Sabías que…?** opens half the gossip in Buenos Aires.', 'draft'),
  ('d16cd641-7bfb-5a9b-9f9c-cea7a142d038', '644dde8f-bffc-57c9-b92e-ec8b078e378c', 'No hace falta que entiendas todo', 'Knowing takes the subjunctive when it''s wanted or needed: **quiero que me expliques** — *I want you to explain it to me*; **no hace falta que entiendas todo** — *you don''t need to understand everything*.', 'draft'),
  ('26ece732-f29e-57ba-91a8-f096c21de744', '41ef8c3c-f27f-547c-8272-9823215b809f', 'Garpar, un afano', 'Money talk goes lunfardo fast: **¿quién garpa?** — *who''s paying?*; **¡es un afano!** — *it''s a rip-off!*; **berreta** — *cheap, shoddy*. And food is **el morfi**.', 'draft'),
  ('71152a01-bffa-5a60-a8d2-60677b67413e', '41ef8c3c-f27f-547c-8272-9823215b809f', 'Chanta, cheto, piola', 'People get lunfardo labels: a **chanta** talks big and doesn''t deliver, a **cheto** is posh, and someone **piola** is cool and easy-going. **No me chamuyes** — *don''t give me that* — is the subjunctive again.', 'draft'),
  ('931d857d-9b5d-5a8a-bb8f-5d48b43e1047', '41ef8c3c-f27f-547c-8272-9823215b809f', 'Qué embole', '**¡Qué embole!** — *what a drag!* — for a rainy Sunday with nothing on. Like all of these: friends, not job interviews.', 'draft'),
  ('23915aa7-29d7-50bf-9702-d626d55a4555', 'c0b981e8-bc40-5a7f-a5cd-1aeb1aeedf2f', 'Reacting to them, to us', '**Me preocupa que trabajes tanto**, **qué suerte que estemos todos**, **me sorprende que no sepa** — a reaction + *que* keeps the subjunctive, for any person.', 'draft'),
  ('0b33b56d-1067-5633-934c-23ee0d2d2252', 'c0b981e8-bc40-5a7f-a5cd-1aeb1aeedf2f', 'Sabía, me di cuenta', '**No sabía** — the state, imperfect. **Me di cuenta** — the moment, preterite. **¿Qué significa *garpar*?** — *to pay*, in lunfardo.', 'draft'),
  ('17486898-58dd-5c51-8771-a3b2dfd4f7a3', '7709eac2-d5bb-5169-aee7-0ed05c8b866e', 'Me voy yendo', 'The porteño exit starts long before the door: **bueno, me voy yendo** — *well, I''d better get going* — and then twenty minutes of talk. On the way out, love for whoever isn''t there: **saludos a tu vieja**, **mandale saludos a Juan**.', 'draft'),
  ('99d583b4-2e71-54bb-8a89-b0ade3db0930', '7709eac2-d5bb-5169-aee7-0ed05c8b866e', 'One more wish', 'A goodbye is a string of wishes, all *que* + the subjunctive: **que duermas bien**, **que sigas bien**, **que llegues bien**. On the phone it ends with **besos** and **escribime**.', 'draft')
